00:06:37.860 --> 00:06:38.540
<v Guest>I believe that.

114
00:06:38.980 --> 00:06:39.500
<v Guest>Yeah.

115
00:06:40.600 --> 00:06:42.620
<v Guest>I spoke with Nolan Lawson,

116
00:06:42.620 --> 00:06:47.140
<v Guest>who wrote a widely read blog post about his experience as a maintainer.

117
00:06:47.380 --> 00:06:50.380
<v Guest>My name is Nolan Lawson. I'm a software engineer.

118
00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:54.000
<v Guest>I'm a JavaScript programmer, JavaScript slinger, basically.

119
00:06:54.340 --> 00:06:56.260
<v Guest>His story is pretty typical.

120
00:06:56.540 --> 00:07:00.160
<v Guest>He got started contributing to a project

121
00:07:00.160 --> 00:07:04.500
<v Guest>and noticed that he was able to help with some open issues.

122
00:07:05.100 --> 00:07:09.400
<v Guest>And the more he helped, the more he found that he enjoyed doing that.

123
00:07:09.780 --> 00:07:26.760
<v Guest>It was just kind of fun to get involved. And then I noticed like, hey, there's all these other, you know, open issues that could also be improved. And so I started kind of working on those. And before I knew it, I was kind of like, you know, one of the top maintainers of the project, kind of like, you know, one of the go-to people for questions and support and things like that.

124
00:07:27.580 --> 00:07:32.420
<v Guest>But unfortunately, it didn't always end up being something joyful for him.

125
00:07:33.240 --> 00:07:43.940
<v Guest>Yeah. So in my post, I use this metaphor of having a line of 100 people standing outside your door, all just kind of patiently waiting for you to let them in and deal with them one at a time.

126
00:07:44.220 --> 00:07:57.120
<v Guest>And, you know, each one is like bringing with you a problem, basically, like like nobody comes to your project and files a bug on you or opens a pull request because everything is fine with the world and they love your project and it perfectly satisfied all their needs.

127
00:07:57.220 --> 00:07:58.800
<v Guest>And they just rode off into the sunset.

128
00:07:58.800 --> 00:08:05.120
<v Guest>said. Oh, gosh, that hit me to my core. I remember when I used to be on the help desk

129
00:08:05.120 --> 00:08:12.580
<v Guest>and when your phone rang, you were filled with dread because you knew someone was calling to

130
00:08:12.580 --> 00:08:17.760
<v Guest>complain about something. And I'm likening that to being a maintainer when he was like,

131
00:08:17.820 --> 00:08:22.260
<v Guest>you open your door and there's 100 people standing in the line. You know, I'm looking at my phone and

132
00:08:22.260 --> 00:08:29.300
<v Guest>every light is lit. It's like, oh my gosh, how overwhelming is that? And that's the first

133
00:08:29.300 --> 00:08:36.100
<v Guest>ingredient in our recipe. An overwhelming or seemingly unending amount of work. That would

134
00:08:36.100 --> 00:08:42.200
<v Guest>be draining enough if it was your day job, which to be clear for many people, it is their day job

135
00:08:42.200 --> 00:08:49.460
<v Guest>to be a maintainer. But for a lot of them, and in Nolan's case, this role is something they do in

136
00:08:49.460 --> 00:08:55.840
<v Guest>in their spare time. Yeah, this was something that I was doing on nights and weekends. And

137
00:08:55.840 --> 00:09:01.680
<v Guest>it got to the point in 2016 where it was basically my only hobby. And I was doing it like every night,

138
00:09:01.760 --> 00:09:08.680
<v Guest>every weekend. That sounds like a lot of non-work work. It does, yeah. And that's ingredient number

139
00:09:08.680 --> 00:09:15.840
<v Guest>two, not having healthy limits or boundaries on your work. Why would he put himself through that?

140
00:09:15.840 --> 00:09:21.100
<v Guest>that. Like the guilt was a huge part of it, right? Because I constantly had people telling me like,

141
00:09:21.120 --> 00:09:26.060
<v Guest>hey, like this bug, like I lost data because of this bug permanently, or like, you know,

142
00:09:26.060 --> 00:09:29.380
<v Guest>my app that I built, like I'm struggling to make it work.

143
00:09:31.300 --> 00:09:36.200
<v Guest>One example that was both encouraging and discouraging was I remember during the Ebola

144
00:09:36.200 --> 00:09:42.160
<v Guest>outbreak in 2015, there was a team that had used our project that used PouchDB to build an app,

145
00:09:42.160 --> 00:09:45.620
<v Guest>and they were actually using it to do like contact tracing out in Africa somewhere.

146
00:09:45.920 --> 00:09:48.380
<v Guest>And it worked, but they ran into problems.

147
00:09:48.540 --> 00:09:50.920
<v Guest>And when they described the problems to me, like the developers told me about the problems,

148
00:09:50.980 --> 00:09:51.900
<v Guest>I thought to myself, like,

149
00:09:51.940 --> 00:09:52.700
<v Clip>I remember that bug.

150
00:09:52.820 --> 00:09:53.320
<v Clip>Yeah,

151
00:09:53.220 --> 00:09:56.000
<v Guest>you know, I knew about that bug, but I didn't really,

152
00:09:56.060 --> 00:09:58.380
<v Guest>I didn't address that bug because I addressed other bugs instead.

153
00:09:58.640 --> 00:10:01.040
<v Guest>And so I actually had to think to myself, like, did this like,

154
00:10:01.200 --> 00:10:03.080
<v Guest>I don't know, did this impede their efforts?

155
00:10:03.200 --> 00:10:06.140
<v Guest>Or should I just be happy that they used it in the first place?

156
00:10:06.560 --> 00:10:08.080
<v Guest>That is heavy. Gosh.

157
00:10:08.080 --> 00:10:12.640
<v Guest>Yeah, that is that is a lot of pressure to put on yourself.

158
00:10:12.860 --> 00:10:18.920
<v Guest>Right. I mean, is he saying that in the back of his head, he's wondering, is this a matter of life and death?

159
00:10:19.680 --> 00:10:27.140
<v Guest>That's very heavy. And to carry that with you because he knew he remembered like, oh, I remember that bug.

160
00:10:27.320 --> 00:10:31.580
<v Guest>But I didn't have time to get to it because there were other things that he had to address.

161
00:10:32.400 --> 00:10:33.300
<v Guest>So, wow.

162
00:10:33.880 --> 00:10:36.820
<v Guest>Yeah. Guilt is a powerful motivator.

163
00:10:38.080 --> 00:10:43.640
<v Guest>really the third ingredient for our recipe for burnout. It's kind of the sense of obligation

164
00:10:43.640 --> 00:10:49.300
<v Guest>to do more of never doing quite enough. We're going to get back to Nolan. There's a bit of

165
00:10:49.300 --> 00:10:55.500
<v Guest>a happy ending there, but Angela's worried. We started off pretty heavy with this episode,

166
00:10:55.640 --> 00:10:59.600
<v Guest>but it's going to get better, I promise. But before it gets better, it's also going to get

167
00:10:59.600 --> 00:11:05.340
<v Guest>a little bit worse. So buckle in, everyone. We're going to talk a little bit about some of the

168
00:11:05.340 --> 00:11:07.780
<v Guest>the other pressures that maintainers can face.

169
00:11:07.980 --> 00:11:11.720
<v Guest>Because apparently the stress of helping even well-meaning people

170
00:11:11.720 --> 00:11:13.280
<v Guest>isn't enough to deal with.

171
00:11:14.000 --> 00:11:16.980
<v Guest>There was a recent study put out by a research group

172
00:11:16.980 --> 00:11:22.260
<v Guest>that looked at the incidence of toxicity in open source communities.

173
00:11:22.600 --> 00:11:24.820
<v Guest>And the reason that they started this study

174
00:11:24.820 --> 00:11:29.120
<v Guest>is because they noticed on blog posts and social medias

175
00:11:29.120 --> 00:11:31.700
<v Guest>and all over the internet that this is a conversation

176
00:11:31.700 --> 00:11:34.560
<v Guest>that was becoming more prominent in the past few years.

177
00:11:35.340 --> 00:11:40.520
<v Guest>they decided to say, hey, you know, we can see all of this anecdotal evidence that something is

178
00:11:40.520 --> 00:11:46.580
<v Guest>happening. Let's do an actual study to see what's going on. Oh, so a lot of people are talking about

179
00:11:46.580 --> 00:11:54.560
<v Guest>this. But as researchers, they want to investigate. They want to put their methodologies behind this

180
00:11:54.560 --> 00:12:02.700
<v Guest>to see what's really going on here, you know. What did they find out? Well, here's Dr. Vasilescu

181
00:12:02.700 --> 00:12:04.760
<v Guest>on the initial findings of their study.

182
00:12:07.020 --> 00:12:08.540
<v Guest>So one thing that we're finding,

183
00:12:08.660 --> 00:12:10.780
<v Guest>and this is largely a confirmation

184
00:12:10.780 --> 00:12:12.740
<v Guest>of what people have talked about,

185
00:12:12.820 --> 00:12:16.900
<v Guest>is that entitlement is a really common theme,

186
00:12:17.000 --> 00:12:19.180
<v Guest>more so than, I don't know,

187
00:12:19.200 --> 00:12:21.400
<v Guest>hate speech or other things like that

188
00:12:21.400 --> 00:12:23.880
<v Guest>that are more prevalent on other platforms.

189
00:12:24.320 --> 00:12:26.740
<v Guest>For those of you who somehow aren't familiar

190
00:12:26.740 --> 00:12:28.120
<v Guest>with comments on the internet,

191
00:12:28.280 --> 00:12:29.800
<v Guest>they can be pretty awful.

192
00:12:30.020 --> 00:12:30.840
<v Guest>You don't say.

193
00:12:32.700 --> 00:12:33.700
<v Guest>And that's really interesting.

194
00:12:33.920 --> 00:12:39.960
<v Guest>So what we mean by entitlement is that often, I guess, people take each other for granted

195
00:12:39.960 --> 00:12:45.140
<v Guest>and make these demands or requests that can be perceived as entitled.

196
00:12:45.520 --> 00:12:51.200
<v Guest>For example, I've opened an issue two hours ago and you still haven't fixed it.

197
00:12:51.900 --> 00:12:53.260
<v Guest>So that's a lot.

198
00:12:53.580 --> 00:12:59.280
<v Guest>And it's a perfect example for the fourth ingredient for burnout, receiving criticism

199
