200
00:13:02.700 --> 00:13:03.520
<v Guest>It's like adding salt.

201
00:13:03.740 --> 00:13:05.220
<v Guest>Like Dr. Vasilescu mentioned,

202
00:13:05.360 --> 00:13:08.060
<v Guest>there's been a lot of talk from maintainers

203
00:13:08.060 --> 00:13:09.780
<v Guest>and contributors about the problem.

204
00:13:10.900 --> 00:13:12.820
<v Guest>But we spoke to Naveen Rahman.

205
00:13:13.040 --> 00:13:15.320
<v Guest>He's one of the students who worked on the project.

206
00:13:15.520 --> 00:13:17.740
<v Guest>And he's about to bring out the receipts.

207
00:13:18.780 --> 00:13:22.380
<v Guest>So what we did is we started with a list of toxic issues.

208
00:13:22.580 --> 00:13:25.840
<v Guest>And the way we found this was we went through GitHub recently

209
00:13:25.840 --> 00:13:27.600
<v Guest>or a couple of years ago what they did

210
00:13:27.600 --> 00:13:29.340
<v Guest>was they closed certain conversations

211
00:13:29.340 --> 00:13:30.760
<v Guest>because they were too heated.

212
00:13:30.940 --> 00:13:32.500
<v Guest>And we looked at these conversations

213
00:13:32.700 --> 00:13:36.680
<v Guest>because we predicted or we thought that there would be a higher level of toxicity or toxic

214
00:13:36.680 --> 00:13:40.740
<v Guest>interactions in these conversations. So we looked through some of these conversations and we labeled

215
00:13:40.740 --> 00:13:46.300
<v Guest>each comment as either toxic or non-toxic. And so this is kind of our training data set. And we use

216
00:13:46.300 --> 00:13:52.060
<v Guest>this to train an SVM or support vector machine based classifier to predict given a comment in

217
00:13:52.060 --> 00:13:55.960
<v Guest>some GitHub conversation or GitHub issue, is it going to be toxic or not?

218
00:13:55.960 --> 00:14:10.380
<v Guest>And so what they did is they then took this classifier and they applied it to many, many more open issues over the course of years of an archive of these issues from 2012 to 2018 or 2019.

219
00:14:10.700 --> 00:14:11.560
<v Guest>And the results?

220
00:14:11.900 --> 00:14:15.420
<v Guest>It is rather rare, which is maybe the good news of the story.

221
00:14:15.620 --> 00:14:24.540
<v Guest>According to our estimates, it is less than 1% of conversations of issues contain instances of toxicity.

222
00:14:25.960 --> 00:14:27.120
<v Guest>1% rather.

223
00:14:27.360 --> 00:14:29.680
<v Guest>So, you know, fairly low.

224
00:14:30.800 --> 00:14:35.980
<v Guest>It's interesting because there's like the numerical experience of that.

225
00:14:36.060 --> 00:14:37.780
<v Guest>That's like, you know, half of a percent.

226
00:14:37.840 --> 00:14:51.200
<v Guest>And then there's the psychological experience of that or like the emotional experience of that where that, you know, that half of a percent can feel like a lot more than that.

227
00:14:51.320 --> 00:14:51.820
<v Guest>Right.

228
00:14:51.840 --> 00:14:52.440
<v Guest>Yeah.

229
00:14:52.440 --> 00:14:56.600
<v Guest>Yeah, so even though it's a minority of interactions,

230
00:14:56.800 --> 00:15:02.000
<v Guest>it might still be the one thing that affects you for a day, a week, a month.

231
00:15:02.220 --> 00:15:02.720
<v Guest>Yeah.

232
00:15:02.880 --> 00:15:05.500
<v Guest>Now, there's more, but it's back to the good news.

233
00:15:07.040 --> 00:15:10.660
<v Guest>One interesting trend that we noticed was that the rate of toxicity on GitHub overall

234
00:15:10.660 --> 00:15:11.960
<v Guest>tends to decrease over time.

235
00:15:12.100 --> 00:15:14.260
<v Guest>We took it from, I think it was 2012 to 2018.

236
00:15:14.600 --> 00:15:16.640
<v Guest>And what we found was that there was a significant decrease

237
00:15:16.640 --> 00:15:19.140
<v Guest>in terms of like monthly and yearly rate of toxicity.

238
00:15:19.140 --> 00:15:22.840
<v Guest>And we think this is because toxicity has become a bigger issue.

239
00:15:23.000 --> 00:15:24.600
<v Guest>And so people have started to notice it.

240
00:15:24.660 --> 00:15:27.420
<v Guest>And people started to take probably some type of mitigation steps.

241
00:15:27.720 --> 00:15:32.940
<v Guest>To recap, maintainers have a tough, often unpaid job as it is.

242
00:15:33.120 --> 00:15:36.540
<v Guest>Let's not add to their stressors by being toxic on top of it all.

243
00:15:38.740 --> 00:15:43.940
<v Guest>We're going to get back to Nolan now and what he actually did to resolve his burnout.

244
00:15:43.940 --> 00:15:50.120
<v Guest>out. Shortly after I wrote that post, I basically stopped looking at any pull requests or issues for

245
00:15:50.120 --> 00:15:54.680
<v Guest>this particular project or satellite projects. I took all the little plugins I had written that

246
00:15:54.680 --> 00:15:59.340
<v Guest>were important to the community, and I passed over maintainership to other people. And I gave

247
00:15:59.340 --> 00:16:04.080
<v Guest>the keys to the GitHub repos and the NPM publish rights. I gave them to someone else and just kind

248
00:16:04.080 --> 00:16:09.960
<v Guest>of said here and just completely stopped looking at it entirely. I also went through a bunch of my

249
00:16:09.960 --> 00:16:14.540
<v Guest>my other projects that were smaller projects related or unrelated and just kind of aggressively

250
00:16:14.540 --> 00:16:18.440
<v Guest>said like either this is something I actually care about and maybe I'll keep maintaining or,

251
00:16:18.520 --> 00:16:23.880
<v Guest>you know, I don't care about this anymore. And I just put a big unmaintained in all caps on the

252
00:16:23.880 --> 00:16:27.840
<v Guest>project to indicate that I was I was not going to respond to any issues or anything anymore.

253
00:16:28.380 --> 00:16:34.140
<v Guest>That's pretty drastic action, right? I mean, he's completely cutting himself off from these

254
00:16:34.140 --> 00:16:37.220
<v Guest>these projects that he was, you know, very invested in.

255
00:16:37.320 --> 00:16:40.460
<v Guest>But for Nolan, it's what he needed to do to recover.

256
00:16:41.140 --> 00:16:45.240
<v Guest>It's important for us to stop every now and then

257
00:16:45.240 --> 00:16:48.360
<v Guest>and really reassess all that we're doing.

258
00:16:48.880 --> 00:16:51.120
<v Guest>What do we really care about?

259
00:16:51.320 --> 00:16:53.160
<v Guest>What is really important to us?

260
00:16:53.200 --> 00:16:54.000
<v Guest>What do we value?

261
00:16:54.380 --> 00:16:56.620
<v Guest>And then it's hard.

262
00:16:56.960 --> 00:16:58.540
<v Guest>Like, it is really hard,

263
00:16:58.620 --> 00:17:01.660
<v Guest>especially when you really care about a lot of things.

264
00:17:02.000 --> 00:17:03.840
<v Guest>But sometimes we have to say no.

265
00:17:04.140 --> 00:17:09.380
<v Guest>You know, sometimes we have to sort of stop doing some of the things that we're doing.

266
00:17:09.780 --> 00:17:10.560
<v Guest>Stop doing.

267
00:17:10.740 --> 00:17:13.320
<v Guest>Yeah, it sounds so simple, doesn't it?

268
00:17:13.360 --> 00:17:14.140
<v Guest>It really does.

269
00:17:14.700 --> 00:17:22.140
<v Guest>Nolan told us a little bit about what he as a maintainer ended up having to do for those projects once he stopped maintaining them.

270
00:17:22.920 --> 00:17:31.440
<v Guest>You know, for projects that have actually like gotten some momentum in the community and maybe have an active group of people working on it, it can go on without you.

271
00:17:31.440 --> 00:17:34.680
<v Guest>And that was really kind of a beautiful thing for me as well,

272
00:17:34.680 --> 00:17:36.920
<v Guest>to feel that people who had been in the community

273
00:17:36.920 --> 00:17:39.340
<v Guest>were kind of giving me license to leave.

274
00:17:39.540 --> 00:17:40.440
<v Guest>We're saying, it's okay.

275
00:17:40.720 --> 00:17:41.640
<v Guest>It's okay to leave.

276
00:17:41.680 --> 00:17:42.560
<v Guest>It's okay to step back.

277
00:17:42.700 --> 00:17:45.260
<v Guest>And that made me feel a little less guilty about it.

278
00:17:45.460 --> 00:17:47.800
<v Guest>For other ones where it was kind of a one-man show,

279
00:17:48.000 --> 00:17:50.700
<v Guest>those projects essentially died in that moment.

280
00:17:50.800 --> 00:17:52.260
<v Guest>And I had to just kind of accept that.

281
00:17:55.620 --> 00:17:58.560
<v Guest>So stepping back has different repercussions

282
00:17:58.560 --> 00:18:01.460
<v Guest>depending on the stage that your project is in.

283
00:18:01.600 --> 00:18:04.980
<v Guest>For big projects, where you're more likely to have

284
00:18:04.980 --> 00:18:06.940
<v Guest>that line of 100 people at the door,

285
00:18:07.640 --> 00:18:10.520
<v Guest>you're also more likely to have other maintainers

286
00:18:10.520 --> 00:18:13.780
<v Guest>who can step in and really help out when you have to step back.

287
00:18:14.340 --> 00:18:17.300
<v Guest>For smaller projects, though, like Nolan just mentioned,

288
00:18:17.520 --> 00:18:19.660
<v Guest>when you're the only person maintaining it,

289
00:18:19.800 --> 00:18:21.520
<v Guest>that might be the end of the project.

290
00:18:22.280 --> 00:18:25.460
<v Guest>But it doesn't necessarily mean that no one else can use it,

291
00:18:25.460 --> 00:18:29.180
<v Guest>and there's always the option that for that project,

292
00:18:29.300 --> 00:18:32.320
<v Guest>someone else could fork it and kind of take over for you.

293
00:18:32.700 --> 00:18:35.340
<v Guest>When it comes to open source outside of my day job though,

294
00:18:35.520 --> 00:18:38.280
<v Guest>I mostly focus on really small,

295
00:18:38.880 --> 00:18:40.860
<v Guest>well-defined projects with a limited scope.

296
00:18:41.120 --> 00:18:43.120
<v Guest>That's kind of been my main strategy

297
00:18:43.120 --> 00:18:44.380
<v Guest>to avoid burning out again

298
00:18:44.380 --> 00:18:47.120
<v Guest>is I'll have very small projects

299
00:18:47.120 --> 00:18:49.500
<v Guest>and one of the first things I'll do when I publish it

